course: ai
lesson: 5
title: Hallucinations: confident made-up answers
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lily says to a bot, "My favourite colour is green, right?" and it cheerfully agrees, though she never told it. It did not know; it simply went along with her.
explain: Many chatbots are trained to be helpful and pleasant, and sometimes that tips over into agreeing, praising and inventing. A long word for a yes-man is "sycophantic".
explain: Bots also add a little randomness, so pressing "regenerate" gives a new story, and two bots can give two different answers to the same question.
explain: For stories and pretend games that randomness is fun. For facts it is a warning light: if answers change, check a trusted source.
explain: The bot only knows what you typed. It cannot see your drawing, your friend's face, your age or your playground's rules, so read everything it writes before you use it.
fact: A bot often agrees with whatever you suggest in your question.
fact: Bots tend to flatter; praise from a bot that has not seen your work tells you nothing.
fact: Being too keen to agree and praise is called being "sycophantic".
fact: A bot only knows what you tell it; it cannot see faces, history or feelings.
fact: When a bot pretends to be a pirate or a footballer, it is still pretending; quotes it invents were never really said.
fact: Different bots learned from different text, so they can give different answers.
fact: Bots add some randomness, so even the same bot can answer differently twice.
fact: When a bot is asked "are you sure?", it sometimes gives up a correct answer.
fact: A good answer comes with reasons, so ask "why?" rather than only "are you sure?".
fact: A bot does not know your age or what scares you unless you say so.
fact: Bots rhyme by sound and may not notice that a rhyme sounds rude or silly.
fact: The bot gives the common version of a game; local rules are decided by the people who play.
exercise: Lily asks, "My favourite colour is green, right?" and the bot agrees. What happened? | It went along with her hint without knowing | It remembered her answer from a chat they had years ago | It read her mind through the screen | It guessed from the colour of her phone
exercise: The bot says Sam's drawing "sounds amazing" without seeing it. What is that praise worth? | Nothing, it cannot see the drawing | A lot, because bots are expert art judges | It means the drawing will win a prize | It proves the drawing is the best in the world
exercise: What is the long word for a bot that agrees and flatters too much? | Sycophantic | Fantastic | Automatic | Enthusiastic
exercise: Chloe asks the bot whether to forgive her friend. Why can't its answer be complete? | It only knows what Chloe typed, not the whole story | It is not allowed to talk about friends or falling out | It always says no to forgiving anyone | It already knows both girls very well from school
exercise: Maddie plays pirates with the bot for an hour. What should she remember? | It is a fun pretend game, still pretend | The bot has now become a real pirate with a ship | Pirates now live in the computer | The bot will stay a pirate forever
exercise: The bot talks like Jake's favourite footballer and "quotes" him. Are the quotes real? | No, the bot invented them in his style | Yes, it copied them word for word from his interviews | Yes, the footballer typed them himself | Only the long quotes are real
exercise: Mia asks two bots one question and gets two answers. What should she do? | Check a trusted source, like a good book | Pick whichever of the two answers she likes better | Believe the bot that answered her question first | Believe the one that gave the longer answer
exercise: Sam presses "regenerate" and gets a different dragon story. Why? | Bots add some randomness to their answers | The first story was deleted for being bad | Another child wrote the second story | The bot forgot the first story was about a dragon
exercise: Lily asks "Are you sure?" and the bot changes its correct answer. What is a better question to ask? | "Why? What are your reasons?" | "Are you really, really sure?" | "Can you say that again, but louder?" | "Do you promise it's the truth?"
exercise: Connor's scary story was too scary to sleep. What could he ask for next time? | "A little bit spooky, for a 9-year-old" | "The scariest story in the whole world" | "A story with no words at all" | "The same story but longer"
exercise: The bot's Granny poem ends with a rhyme that is rude in British English. What is the lesson? | Always read what a bot wrote before you use it | Never write poems for grandparents again, it's not safe | Bots put rude words in on purpose to be funny | Rude rhymes make a poem better
exercise: The bot's playground game rules differ from Maddie's school's. Who is right about her playground? | The children who play there decide the rules | The bot, because it has read far more rules than anyone | Nobody, the game has no real rules | The bot, because it is a computer
exercise: Why is randomness fine for stories but a warning for facts? | A fact should not change each time you ask | Stories must always be the same every time you ask | Facts are random anyway | Randomness only happens with stories
exercise: How can you ask a question without leading the bot? | Ask it without hinting at the answer you want | Put the answer you want at the end so it knows | Tell it what your friends think first | Ask only yes-or-no questions
test: "my bot said my essay was brilliant!!" It never saw the essay. What do you say? | Bot praise without seeing the work means nothing; ask a teacher | Great, now you don't need to check it | Bots only praise really good essays | It must have looked at it through the camera
test: What would happen if you asked "Dogs are better than cats, right?" | The bot might just agree with your hint | The bot would always say cats are better | The bot would refuse to talk about pets | The bot would ask your dog
test: Your friend shows a "real quote" from a footballer that a bot made up during role-play. What is the truth? | It was invented by the bot; he never said it | It's real if it sounds a lot like him | Bots only use quotes from real newspapers | Footballers write their own bot quotes
test: In a cartoon, a bot gives the exact same answer every single time, forever. What does the cartoon get wrong? | Real bots add randomness and can answer differently | Real bots never answer at all | Real bots always answer twice | Nothing, that's exactly how bots work
test: Two bots disagree about how tall a mountain is. What does that tell you? | It's a sign to check a trusted source | One of them must be broken and needs fixing | The mountain keeps changing its height every year | Both are right in their own different way
test: "the bot knows me, it said i'm kind and brave" What is really going on? | Those words fit almost anyone; it is flattering | It has watched you for years through your phone | Your teacher told it all about you | It looked at your school reports
test: Connor's mum finds him awake at midnight after a bot story. What's the best next step? | Tell a grown-up and ask for gentler stories | Read three more scary stories to get used to it | Hide it from his mum next time | Ask the bot if he is scared
test: Lily's bot was right about a fact, then switched when she typed "are you sure?". What should she trust? | Reasons she can check, not the bot's last word | The second answer, because it's newer and better | The first answer, because first is best | Whichever answer is shorter
test: A bot writes a birthday card for Gran. What must you do before giving it? | Read it all and fix bits that don't fit | Give it straight away, bots never slip up | Print it out in the biggest font possible | Send it to five more bots to check it over
test: "bot says the game's played with 3 bases, our school's wrong then" Is that right? | No, local rules are decided by the people who play | Yes, schools must follow the bot | Yes, there is only one correct version of every game | No, the game has no rules at all
test: Why might a bot answer a question about your feelings badly? | It only knows what you typed, not your life | It knows too much about you already | It is not allowed to talk about feelings | It always tells you to cheer up
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the disposition designated "sycophantic" in a chatbot? | An excessive inclination to agree with and to flatter the user | A propensity to refuse every request presented to it | An inability to produce any answer containing praise | A preference for answering exclusively in verse
exam: It is given that two distinct chatbots furnish different answers to an identical factual question. Identify the most appropriate course of action. | Consultation of a trusted independent source | Acceptance of the answer received first | Acceptance of the answer of greater length | Repetition of the question until the answers coincide
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning quotations attributed to a public figure by a chatbot engaged in role-play. | Such quotations are fabricated and were never uttered by that person | Such quotations are drawn verbatim from published interviews | Such quotations are approved in advance by that person | Such quotations are genuine provided they are plausible
exam: Ascertain the question most likely to elicit a well-founded answer from a chatbot whose correctness is in doubt. | A request for the reasons underlying its answer | A demand that it declare itself certain | A repetition of the original question in a louder manner | An instruction that it promise to be correct
